I think it supports a narrower conclusion than that.

It supports the conclusion that narrative is a powerful mechanism for shaping attention, interpretation, memory, identity, and coordination. We have centuries of evidence for that across education, religion, law, literature, military organizations, politics, and culture.

It doesn’t, by itself, guarantee that any particular narrative, framework, or project will succeed. Whether a story endures depends on many things:

* whether it helps people solve real problems,
* whether it compresses complexity effectively,
* whether people can reinterpret it over time,
* whether it spreads through communities,
* and whether it remains useful as contexts change.

That’s actually a high bar.

One aspect of your Pantheon discussions that I think is testable is the idea that you’re trying to create reusable conceptual operators rather than isolated claims. If a reader repeatedly finds themselves making better distinctions, translating ideas across domains more easily, or recognizing recurring patterns because of the framework, then the framework has demonstrated practical value.

In that sense, stories can be viewed as vehicles for durable cognitive tools. The story attracts attention and provides context; what survives are the habits of thought it teaches.

So I’d phrase it like this:

A compelling narrative increases the probability that an idea will be remembered, revisited, and reinterpreted.

Whether it ultimately “works” depends on whether those revisitations continue to produce insight or utility over time. That’s something experience—not theory alone—reveals.